<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Edward Jenner</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1749">1749</a>–<a href="/years/1823">1823</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Edward Jenner was an English physician and scientist, born in Berkeley, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gloucestershire">Gloucestershire</a>, who created the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smallpox-vaccine">smallpox vaccine</a>, the world's first vaccine. Having observed that cowpox appeared to protect against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smallpox">smallpox</a>, on 14 May 1796 he inoculated James Phipps, the eight-year-old son of his gardener, with pus from the cowpox blisters of the milkmaid Sarah Nelmes. In 1798 he described the method in his Inquiry into the Variolae vaccinae known as the Cow Pox, and the words vaccine and vaccination come from his term for cowpox. He was appointed physician to King George IV in 1821 and was among the first to describe the brood parasitism of the cuckoo.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/edward-jenner.jpg" alt="Edward Jenner" width="330" height="417" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: John Raphael Smith · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Edward_Jenner.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Edward Jenner was born on 17 May 1749 in Berkeley, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gloucestershire">Gloucestershire</a>, the eighth of nine children of the Reverend Stephen Jenner, the vicar of Berkeley. He was inoculated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/variolation">variolation</a> during his schooling, which had a lifelong effect on his health. At 13 he was apprenticed for seven years to the surgeon Daniel Ludlow of Chipping Sodbury, and in 1770 he became apprenticed in surgery and anatomy under John Hunter at St George's Hospital in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>. By 1773 he was back in Berkeley as a successful family doctor and surgeon, and in 1792 he obtained the degree of MD from the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-andrews">St Andrews</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Jenner was elected a fellow of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-society">Royal Society</a> in 1788 after publishing a study of the nesting cuckoo that combined observation, experiment and dissection. He showed that the newly hatched cuckoo, not the adult, pushes the host's eggs and chicks from the nest, and he described a depression in its back, gone after about twelve days, that lets it cup them. In March 1788 he married Catherine Kingscote, with whom he had three children.</p><p>Others had tried cowpox before him. John Fewster realised by 1768 that cowpox gave immunity, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dorset">Dorset</a> farmer Benjamin Jesty vaccinated his wife and two children in 1774. Donald Hopkins wrote that Jenner's unique contribution was to prove by later challenge that those inoculated were immune, and that the protective material could pass from person to person. After inoculating James Phipps, Jenner injected him with variolous material on 1 July 1796, and no disease followed. He then tested 23 further subjects. Parliament granted him £10,000 in 1802, and in 1840 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/variolation">variolation</a> was banned and vaccination offered free.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Jenner was found with his right side paralysed on 25 January 1823 and died the next day, aged 73, of an apparent stroke. He was buried in the family vault at the Church of St Mary, Berkeley. The Spanish Balmis Expedition of 1803 to 1806 carried the vaccine to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a>, Macao and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> had his troops vaccinated. In 1980 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-health-organization">World Health Organization</a> declared <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smallpox">smallpox</a> eradicated, with vaccination an essential component. His house at Berkeley is now a small museum.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Edward Jenner” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
